NAME
    wmterm - terminal-emulator WM client (Path C phase 27)

SYNOPSIS
    wmterm [-v] [SECONDS]

DESCRIPTION
    Open a 540x240 WM window hosting a 60x24 grid of text and
    spawn sh.elf inside a PTY whose master end is the wmterm
    process and whose slave is the shell's stdin/stdout/stderr.

    The window forwards every keystroke (WM_EV_KEY) into the
    master fd, so the focused window's keyboard ends up at the
    shell exactly as if it were a "real" terminal.  Anything
    the shell writes to stdout / stderr goes through the master
    fd back out and into the local text grid.

    `-v` enables per-event diagnostic prints (FOCUS / UNFOCUS /
    KEY / read) to the launching console — useful for tracing
    WM event delivery, off by default so a normal session
    doesn't spam its parent terminal.

    The grid is repainted every frame in light-green text on a
    near-black background.  A blinking white caret marks the
    cursor.  ANSI escape sequences (CSI '... <final>') are
    stripped — colour codes are recognised but ignored; the
    terminal renders plain characters only.

    Special key handling (kernel keyboard layer):
      Enter           sends '\r' / '\n' (depending on TTY mode)
      Backspace       sends 0x7F (DEL)
      Arrow keys      come through as ESC '[' 'A'/'B'/'C'/'D'

    Mouse interactions happen at the WM level (drag title bar,
    resize from corner, close X) and don't reach the shell.

    Exit after SECONDS (default 120) or when the WM closes the
    window.  Closing wmterm leaves the child sh.elf running
    until its own EOF on stdin — there's no SIGHUP wiring yet.

HISTORY
    Session 157 wired sys_read(FD_PTY_M/_S) to honor FD_FL_NONBLOCK
    so wmterm's main loop can drain its event queue every tick
    without blocking on a quiet shell.  Before that fix, wmterm
    appeared to "hang" — typing and the close X never reached it
    because wm_poll_event ran at most once per tick, and the read
    after it blocked forever.

